\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9\lquote It was mainly for economic reasons that Britain expanded its empire in Africa between 1870 and 1914.\rquote  Assess the validity of this view [25 marks]\par
\par
The economic priorities of Britain (government, businesses and individuals) lay behind much of the colonial expansion in Africa between 1870 and 1914. The search for secure markets in the face of growing protectionism in Europe, and the hunt for raw materials, such as gold and palm oil, can both be discerned as motives for Britain\rquote s participation in the \lquote Scramble for Africa\rquote , as can the desire to protect valuable British investments there. Yet, a narrow focus on economics is too crude an explanation: it misses the complex ways in which economics interacted with other motives such as strategic concerns about the actions of Britain\rquote s European rivals, as well as the way in which unpredictable events in the periphery often seemed to force action. While economics is an important consideration, this rapid phase of colonisation defies simplistic analysis; no general explanatory rule can be established for the entirety of Britain\rquote s African empire.\par
\par
It is certainly possible to identify some economic motives for expansion. The dated, but influential, JA Hobson (1902) argued that businessmen lay behind British imperial policy which he characterised as \lquote primarily a struggle for profitable markets for investment.\rquote  Lenin (1917) built on this claim with his assertion that imperialism was \lquote the highest stage of capitalism.\rquote  The onset of the Great Depression in the 1870s, and the following erection of tariff barriers by Germany and other European states, created concern in Britain that the era of free trade was drawing to a close. Direct control of territory was now seen as a crucial means of securing access to markets for British industry. However, it would be more accurate to say that much of this drive to secure markets was speculative; in the 1890s Britain did more trade with Belgium than with the whole of sub-Saharan Africa. Unlike India, Britain\rquote s African colonies never became enormous export markets for British industry and so pursuit of markets was not the main reason for expansion in Africa.\par
\par
More plausible is the claim that Africa was becoming an increasingly important source of raw materials. The growing importance of palm oil as a lubricant for British industry drove George Goldie\rquote s Royal Niger Company to expand its control over Nigeria in the 1880s and 1890s. The export of cocoa, an increasingly lucrative cash crop, was an important consideration in explaining Britain\rquote s consolidation of control over the Gold Coast in the 1890s and 1900s. Most obviously, the desire to secure control over the diamond mines of Kimberley and the gold mines of the Transvaal were important causes of the two waves of expansion in South Africa, in the 1870s and 1890s respectively. Speculation that the land was rich in precious metals also partly lay behind Cecil Rhodes\rquote  conquest of what would become known as the Rhodesias in the 1880s and 1890s.\par
\par
Yet, exploitation of raw materials often came after colonisation and was not the direct motive for it. It was only once Kenya was under firm direct rule that settlers arrived in the Kikuyu highlands in Kenya to farm the fertile soil they found there. Southern Rhodesia would disappoint in its mining potential and developed into a primarily agricultural economy (although Northern Rhodesia did later become an important exporter of copper). In Sudan (and, indeed, in Egypt itself), cotton became a hugely important export feeding the mills of Lancashire. However, the massive expansion of irrigation and the cotton industry came only after British occupation and were not the reasons for British rule in the first place.\par
\par
Certainly, Britain was also sometimes driven to act to protect the interests of investors. Cain and Hopkins\rquote  theory of \lquote gentlemanly capitalism\rquote  places City financiers as the key group putting pressure on ministers to annexe territory in the late 19th century. This is most obviously seen in Egypt in 1882 when a reluctant William Gladstone felt he had no option other than to occupy the country to protect European investments from the clutches of Arabi Pasha and his anti-European nationalist army. The hand of investors has also been identified behind the Boer War of 1899, where the influence of the so-called \lquote Rand Millionaires\rquote  put pressure on Alfred Milner to toughen his stance towards the Transvaal. Yet, the interests of investors cannot be seen as causing the majority of imperial acquisitions in Africa, most of which received tiny amounts of European investment well into the 20th century.\par
\par
Britain\rquote s strategic concerns were a more immediate cause of much of the colonial expansion between 1870 and 1914; Britain feared the encroachments of its rivals, that their expansion might threaten Britain\rquote s security, as well as its broader economic interests. Brendan Simms has identified Africa as \lquote the fulcrum of European geopolitics\rquote  in this period. This is certainly how the German Chancellor, Otto von Bismarck, viewed colonial acquisitions; African possessions were potential bargaining chips that could be used to advance a country\rquote s interests in the great power diplomacy of the pre-1914 period. There are several examples of territories that had negligible economic importance in themselves, but that were taken purely for their strategic significance. Britain\rquote s return to the Sudan under Kitchener in 1898 was primarily to forestall Jean-Baptiste Marchand\rquote s French expedition to Fashoda and to ensure that the upper Nile would not fall into the hands of a rival power and threaten Egypt. Sudan had no serious economic value to anyone in Britain at the time. Indeed, Egypt itself was more important for its strategic route to India (the Suez Canal) than it was for its intrinsic economic value. It is doubtful that Gladstone would have occupied the country in 1882 simply to help British investors, were it not for the presence of the canal, although these issues cannot be fully disentangled from one another. Notwithstanding the economic value of palm oil from Nigeria, the expansion north up the Niger, into the Islamic territory of the Sokoto caliphate, was more about marking out the boundaries with French territory than it was about direct economic benefit. \par
\par
Fear of encroaching rivals can be detected in southern Africa too. Bechuanaland became a British protectorate in 1885 simply to ensure that Germany would not expand there, linking up with the Transvaal and splitting the Cape from Britain\rquote s territory north of the Zambezi. The Boer War was partly caused by a fear that the Transvaal\rquote s growing economic clout, and its closeness to Germany (demonstrated by the Kruger Telegram of 1896), would threaten Britain\rquote s control over the Cape, which remained of vital strategic importance on the route to India. Nyasaland was declared a protectorate in 1889 because Scottish missionaries there feared Portuguese expansion from neighbouring Mozambique. Its economic value at the time was non-existent, with no railway to the coast constructed to export its limited agricultural produce until 1907. \par
\par
Finally, Gallagher and Robinson\rquote s argument that British decision makers were haphazardly responding to events in Africa itself is worth considering. As they have argued, it was the rise of Egyptian nationalism that prompted the 1882 occupation, and it was the growing power of the Transvaal that led to the Boer War of 1899. Instability created by Ashanti expansion led to a series of wars that expanded British rule in the Gold Coast, culminating in the final defeat of the Ashanti in 1900. Civil war and religious upheaval in Buganda prompted annexation by the British East Africa Company in 1899, while the volatility caused by the Mahdist regime in the Sudan was one of the reasons that drew Britain back into that country. Nevertheless, in all these cases the British acted because they felt their economic or strategic interests were threatened; although the timing was dictated by events in the periphery, the primary motives for expansion can be found in the metropole.\par
\par
Thus, economics must be seen as an important reason for British expansion between 1870 and 1914. However, it is an insufficient explanation in itself. As has been demonstrated, economic justification was often flimsy and speculative although, in certain cases, could prove decisive in prompting the British to act to secure their interests. In the context of the imbalance of military power between European and African societies in the late 19th century this action could often be relatively straightforward and cost-free. However, fear of rivals and a determination to secure land that had strategic importance was often the immediate reason why Britain acquired territory during the \lquote scramble\rquote . Although wider economic interests might be discerned lurking behind Britain\rquote s strategic considerations, it is simply not convincing to portray British expansion in Africa as solely a direct outcome of economic interests.\par
